    Milestone 10 (#10)
    
    * Milestone 10 step 1: the prose that had not followed the code
    
    Four passages two milestones stale, and a runner table that had never
    listed signal(): CONTRIBUTING named fan-out and compensation as deferred,
    the retry contract said compensation was out of scope, the README said
    the connectors stay experimental until a release that happened in
    October, and SECURITY.md's supported table named 0.1.x.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
    
    * Milestone 10 step 2: an ambient execution scope, and the per-tenant rate-limit bucket
    
    The Milestone 7 audit finding Milestone 8 deferred here: the connector
    layer had no view of the executing tenant. ExecutionScope is the record
    of which execution this process is running — entered under the claim,
    left from a finally, nesting like the log context it now drives —
    and RateLimitPolicy gains a scope, connection or tenant, so a shared
    account's ceiling is counted per tenant of the running execution. The
    tenant is read from durable state, never from the request. Outside a
    tenant the connection's budget applies: a scope narrows, it does not
    invent.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
    
    * Milestone 10 step 3: declare the cron-expression dependency, and record the leftovers
    
    illuminate/console only suggests dragonmantank/cron-expression; every
    Laravel application has it through laravel/framework, but a package
    relying on that relies on a dependency it never declared. Required at
    ^3.4, the constraint both supported majors share.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
    
    * Milestone 10 step 4: the contracts — schedules, triggers, tenant ceilings
    
    Semantics before code, as every milestone since the seventh. A tick is
    a row whose unique (schedule, instant) index is the once-per-tick
    guarantee, committed with the execution it starts; a trigger is a
    signed POST bound to timestamp, execution, signal, and body, verified
    before any read, with no environment bypass and no inbox; a tenant
    ceiling lives in claim() and nowhere else, honest about the instant,
    exact in the steady state, fairness stated as a sizing rule.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
    
    * Milestone 10 step 5: the schedule tables
    
    Two new tables, the tenth and eleventh post-freeze migrations.
    laraflow_schedules is one row per registered schedule — a cache of the
    declaring class, synchronised every run; what only the row holds is
    last_tick_at, the high-water mark before which nothing is due.
    laraflow_schedule_ticks is one insert-only row per tick, and its unique
    (schedule_id, tick_at) index is the once-per-tick guarantee: every
    scheduler names the same instant, exactly one insert wins. A tick's
    execution id is a plain column, not a foreign key, so tick history
    outlives what retention deletes — the sub-workflow links' trade-off.
    Nothing reads the tables yet; the scheduler lands in the steps that
    follow.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
    
    * Milestone 10 steps 6-7: schedules — the declaration, the registry, and the scheduler
    
    A workflow declares its cadence by returning a Schedule from the new
    optional schedule(); listing the class activates it, and a package
    registers through ScheduleRegistry — both paths end in one registry.
    The scheduler synchronises declarations to rows, names the due tick
    (a pure function of expression, timezone, and clock, so every reader
    of a minute names the same instant), claims it by inserting its row
    under the unique index, creates the execution in the claiming
    transaction, and dispatches after the commit through the same Handoff
    the console uses. Skip counts the gap on the tick that runs; CatchUp
    replays oldest first, each execution told its own tick, bounded per
    run and by a window; withoutOverlapping claims the tick and records
    what it declined to run beside; a lost push is healed by the next run,
    which may re-dispatch what its own ticks started because a scheduled
    execution is never work the application was holding.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
    
    * Milestone 10 step 8: the scheduling console
    
    laraflow:schedule is the tick run — one row per schedule decision,
    non-zero when a person is needed, --dry-run naming what it would claim
    and claiming nothing, --sync for the application with no queue.
    laraflow:schedules lists every schedule with its last tick, the lag it
    was claimed at, the next due instant, and its state, and exits non-zero
    on an orphan or a stall — due ticks standing unclaimed, the signature
    of a scheduler that stopped. The same stalled condition is an attention
    line in laraflow:summary and the dashboard overview through the one
    AttentionScan both read, and the summary no longer sleeps through an
    empty table when something is wrong, because a stalled schedule with no
    executions is exactly what a dead scheduler leaves. laraflow:show and
    the execution page name the tick that started a scheduled execution.
    --forget is for orphans only: a live schedule's history must not be
    deletable by typo.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
    
    * Milestone 10 step 9: the race, proven at the claim and across processes
    
    Two proofs of once-per-tick. In the suite: two schedulers read the same
    high-water mark — the real interleaving, a decision made from a read
    the winner's commit postdates — both create their execution inside the
    claiming transaction, and the loser's transaction rolls back whole,
    execution and plan rows included, leaving one tick, one execution, and
    a mark that advanced exactly once. In CI: the install walk races three
    real laraflow:schedule processes at one due tick on every engine and
    requires one started, one execution, three zero exits — losing the
    insert is the designed outcome, so it must not page anyone. SQLite gets
    WAL and a busy timeout for the race; the engines need neither.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
    
    * Milestone 10 steps 10-12: triggers — the signed endpoint, the scheme, and named triggers
    
    Signals over HTTP, opt-in: Triggers::routes() mounts nothing until
    called, and refuses at the mount when no usable key is configured,
    because an endpoint that could verify nothing would refuse everything
    and look like a bug. The signed endpoint verifies before it reads —
    HMAC-SHA256 over timestamp, execution id, signal, and body hash, bound
    to the target and independent of the path — and answers every
    verification failure the same way, 401 and nothing more; the reason
    goes to the log and the TriggerRefused event. A verified request is the
    dashboard's signal action answered as JSON: the same signal(), the same
    Handoff, 202 with what became of the execution, 404 only after
    verification so nothing can enumerate, 409/413/422 in the runtime's own
    words. TriggerSignature::sign() signs with the primitives the endpoint
    verifies with, so the two cannot drift. Named triggers mount a provider
    endpoint behind the provider's own TriggerVerifier and the
    application's mapping to one SignalIntent or nothing; everything after
    resolution is the signed endpoint's path. No schema, no inbox, no
    signature store: replay is bounded by the signal contract and the
    tolerance, and the residual window is written down with its defences.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
    
    * Milestone 10 step 13: the trigger exit criterion over real HTTP
    
    The install walk parks a real approval workflow on a signal, mounts the
    endpoint from a provider with a key from the environment, and drives it
    with curl: unsigned is 401 with the execution still waiting, a GET is
    405, a request signed with nothing but openssl — the recipe
    docs/triggers.md gives any non-PHP caller — is 202, the worker resumes
    the execution with the payload, and a replay after the resume is 409
    because nothing is waiting on it any more.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
    
    * Milestone 10 step 13a: what CI found
    
    Two findings from the first run of the matrix against Phase D. The
    install walk's dashboard-absence stage asserted that no route named
    laraflow.* existed, written when the dashboard was the only route
    owner; the trigger stage before it now mounts laraflow.triggers.*, so
    the assertion names the dashboard's own routes instead — triggers and
    the dashboard are mounted independently, and one being present says
    nothing about the other. And two trigger tests compared a payload read
    back from a JSON column with assertSame: MySQL keeps JSON object keys
    in its own order, the lesson of Milestone 8 step 13a, so the keys are
    compared and the order is not.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
    
    * Milestone 10 steps 14-15: tenant ceilings, enforced at the claim
    
    Every run begins at claim(), so a ceiling checked there is checked on
    every path — a fresh dispatch, a due retry, a resumed wait, a heartbeat,
    recovery — and nothing else needs to know it exists. max_concurrent
    counts the tenant's executions holding a worker right now: running or
    compensating with a fresh claim, because a stale claim is a dead
    worker's and a dead worker holds no slot; max_claims_per_minute counts
    churn through the cache-backed limiter the connector throttle already
    uses. A claim a ceiling turns away is deferred, not failed: status
    unchanged, no attempt recorded, deferred_until and a deferral count on
    the row (the twelfth post-freeze migration, with the index behind the
    count and one behind the summary), cleared by the first claim that
    lands. The queue job re-pushes for that instant without spending a try;
    an in-process caller is told; the pending scan waits for the timer;
    show, summary, and the dashboard say deferred ×N, next at. Every value
    ships off, an execution without a tenant is never bounded, and there is
    no operator override — raising the ceiling is the override, at the
    right scope. Honest about the instant, exact in the steady state.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
    
    * Milestone 10 step 16: the fairness rule, under test
    
    Four simulated workers take work in arrival order — the flooding
    tenant's whole backlog first, then the other tenant's five — with the
    flood held to two slots. The other tenant finishes in the same number
    of rounds whether the flood is twenty executions or two hundred,
    asserted as equality, because 'roughly the same' is how a dependence
    on the backlog hides; and the flood never holds more than its ceiling
    in any round. Beside it: the count excluding parked, pending, and stale
    executions; a refusal writing the deferral and nothing else; the first
    landing claim clearing it; the rate ceiling bounding churn with nothing
    running; no tenant, no bound; an override lifting a ceiling by naming
    null; a custom policy asked on every claim; the job re-pushing for the
    deferral with tries untouched and the sync driver surfacing the
    refusal; the pending scan honouring the timer; and the event, the show
    line, the execute refusal, and the summary line.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
    
    * Milestone 10 steps 17-19: laraflow:signal, laraflow:children, and bulk cancel and re-run
    
    laraflow:signal is the console form of the dashboard's signal action
    and of a trigger request, through the same signal() and the same
    Handoff; recording is not delivery, and --deliver hands the execution
    on in the same breath. laraflow:children lists a sub-workflow's child
    or a fan-out's members as the page laraflow:list prints — the table and
    the next-page hint moved into one concern so the two cannot disagree
    about what a page looks like. laraflow:cancel and laraflow:execute
    without an id act on what a filter matches, oldest first, up to
    --limit: a filter is required, because a flag-less command that acts
    on everything is a typo away from an incident; the production
    confirmation applies unless --force; --dry-run names and changes
    nothing; and each row is its own attempt, so a refusal or a repeat
    failure never stops the rest. The output plumbing every command will
    share — one JSON serialiser, one envelope, one refusal path — lands
    here because the new commands are built on it.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
    
    * Milestone 10 step 20: --json on every operating command
    
    One document on standard output, nothing else, the human form's exit
    code: command, ok, and error on a refusal, with every record serialised
    through one class so no field is spelled two ways. The installer and
    the generators are scaffolding and stay human. A bulk operation or a
