    * Milestone 9 steps 2 and 3: the fan-out and compensation contracts, decided before built
    
    docs/advanced-workflows.md specified Milestone 7's three capabilities
    before any of them existed and has been their shipped contract since.
    Milestone 9's two are specified the same way, in the same document,
    before a line of either is written.
    
    Fan-out is composition widened, not a new mechanism: a member is a
    child execution with the same linkage, depth bound, cascade, and
    retention predicates a sub-workflow's child has. What the design has
    to answer for is cardinality. The member list is read once and
    recorded, so the group is a durable fact rather than something
    recomputed on every claim; creation is chunked and resumable, with the
    declared count as the fact that makes a partial creation completable;
    the join is a wait on one reserved signal, heartbeating as a single
    child's park does; and the group's progress is one indexed aggregate,
    because counting a thousand members by reading them would defeat the
    purpose. That aggregate is a deliberate addition to the public
    contract under the Milestone 4 rule, not a shortcut.
    
    The join waits for all. WaitAll is the only policy that ships:
    cancelling siblings on a first failure is a destructive reaction to a
    partial failure, it races every member already mid-step, and nothing
    has asked for it — so the enum carries one case and adding the second
    later is additive rather than a signature change.
    
    A compensation is an ordinary step row appended after the plan,
    carrying a link to the row it undoes. That one decision buys attempts,
    retries, timeouts, the attempt history, laraflow:show, and the
    dashboard without a line of new machinery — "inspectable like any
    other" becomes true by construction. The plan is then the rows that
    compensate nothing, which is how validation keeps comparing like with
    like. It is opt-in by declaring it, runs only on terminal failure,
    skips the step that failed because a step that threw is in a state
    only the application knows, and stops at the first compensation whose
    retries are exhausted rather than reaching past it to undo work the
    failed one may have depended on.
    
    Neither a fan-out nor a sub-workflow compensates its children. A
    parent reaching into executions it does not own is the hidden
    authority the shared principles forbid, and there is deliberately no
    setting that turns it on; an author who wants it writes it, with the
    member ids the output recorded.
    
    ExecutionStatus gains Compensating — the first case added since that
    enum promised more might be, which is the promise now being kept.
    
