    * Milestone 9 step 4: a join counts its members in one indexed query
    
    ExecutionRepository::childProgress() returns the children of one
    parent step counted by status, as a ChildProgress value object that
    carries counts and nothing else. A fan-out's join asks this on every
    heartbeat, and a group may be a thousand wide; reading the members to
    count them would make the heartbeat cost what it exists to avoid. The
    children are found by the parent step, not the parent execution: a
    workflow may fan out at several positions, and each join must see only
    its own.
    
    It is served by the eighth post-freeze migration, an index on
    parent_step_execution_id — which Milestone 7 had left unindexed, and
    said so, because it was only ever read for one known child found
    through the parent_execution_id index and a comparison in PHP. A
    thousand members read that way is a thousand rows walked to answer
    "are you done". The freeze test learns the new index and the new
    file's hash; the upgrade harness rolls back one step further and
    checks the index is gone and then back.
    
    A deliberate addition to the public contract under the Milestone 4
    rule — a view needs something the contract cannot answer, so the
    contract gains it with its index — rather than a shortcut for the
    join. Breaking for a custom ExecutionRepository, which must implement
    it; the crash-simulating double that stands in for one does.
    
    "None yet" is not "all done": a step with no children answers an
    empty progress whose allFinished() is false, because a join with
    nothing to join is empty, and the step decides what that means.
    
