    * Milestone 9 step 5: a step fans out to many child executions and parks on their join
    
    FanOutStep::members() yields a FanOut — a workflow and its input — per
    member. Composition widened, not a new mechanism: a member is a child
    execution with the same linkage, depth bound, cancellation cascade, and
    retention predicates a sub-workflow's child has, and the plan is
    untouched. What the design answers for is cardinality.
    
    members() is read once. What it yielded is recorded on the step row
    before any member is created — on the row's input column, which the
    package had never written after creation and never read, and which is
    exactly "what the step was given to do". Every create() commits on its
    own, so a worker that dies anywhere leaves exactly the members it made,
    and the next claim counts them and makes the remainder; a test kills
    the delivery on the second member and proves the third is made later
    and members() is not read again. Building that found the runner's own
    checkpoint clobbering the declaration: the runner and the machinery
    hold separate handles for the same row, and the runner's carries no
    input, so the repository now writes input only when a handle carries
    one — a declaration is written once and never erased.
    
    The step parks on laraflow.join.{stepRowId}. The last member to rest
    records it, with the group's counts, and asks for the parent: the
    notifier reads the parent step's own wait to know whether the child is
    one of one or one of many, which costs two queries more than a single
    child needs and avoids a log line per member. The park heartbeats as a
    single child's does — building that found park() scheduling a
    heartbeat for a child signal alone, so a join parked indefinitely;
    every machinery signal heartbeats now. On the sync driver the group
    runs inline and the step completes without parking.
    
    One class, FanOutGroup, answers what the members' state means — for
    the step on creation and retry, for the resolver on every heartbeat,
    and for the notifier when a member rests — so the three cannot
    disagree. Counts first, from the one indexed aggregate; rows only when
    a decision needs them. The depth walk a sub-workflow and a fan-out
    share moves to Nesting so the two cannot count differently.
    
    Width is bounded by laraflow.workflows.max_members, default 1000,
    refused before anything is created and checked as members() is read
    so one that never ends is refused at the bound rather than exhausting
    memory first.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
    
    * Milestone 9 step 6: the join waits for every member, and a retry resumes rather than re-charges
    
    WaitAll is the only policy that ships, and the tests now say what it
    means. A member that fails does not fail the step while its siblings
    are still going: the heartbeat that comes due with one failure and two
    members outstanding parks again rather than failing on the one it can
    see. When the whole group has rested, every member completed means the
    step completes with the summary, and anything else fails it with
    FanOutFailedException — how many ended in each state, and the first
    five that did not complete, with "and more" when there are.
    
    That failure is an ordinary step failure under the step's retry
    policy, and the retry does what the contract promised: a member whose
    failure predates the attempt is the attempt's work, not its answer, so
    it is handed back to a worker and the step parks again until it rests,
    charged nothing; the same members, not siblings. A cancelled member
    cannot be resumed, so a group containing one reports the same outcome
    on every retry until the policy is exhausted or an operator intervenes
    — and is never handed to a worker again.
    
    Writing the sync-driver case found the gap: a member's own failure
    surfaces inside the parent's delivery call, and it was escaping as the
    parent's exception rather than being one member's recorded answer for
    the group to decide on. It is contained now, and so is a push that
    fails on any driver, which leaves the member pending for the heartbeat
    and is logged rather than failing the fan-out. Containing it changed
    what the step-5 resume test could simulate: a thrown delivery is no
    longer a death, so the test uses the suite's crash double instead and
    dies on the second member's create — nothing after it persisted, the
    parent still claimed — and a live worker takes over the stale claim
    six minutes later and makes the remainder without reading members()
    again. That is the shape a real death has, and the test is truer for
    it.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
    
    * Milestone 9 steps 7 and 8: cancellation reaches every member, and nothing can leave a group hanging
    
    The cascade already paged through children rather than loading them,
    so cancelling a group was correct — but not quiet. Each cancelled
    member told its parent it had finished, which for a fan-out meant
    three queries and, since the parent was itself cancelled and could not
    receive a signal, a refusal and a log line. Twenty-five members, twenty
    -five lines; a thousand, a thousand. The notifier now reads the
    parent once and stops there when it is not parked: it has not parked
    yet, or it is cancelled, or it finished without us, and the heartbeat
    covers every one of those. Quieter for a single child too.
    
    The heartbeat's self-healing is now held by tests rather than by
    argument. A join signal lost between a member's release and the
    parent's write: the heartbeat consults the record and completes the
    step anyway. A member whose dispatch never reached a worker: it is
    re-delivered and the members that ran are not. Members pruned by
    retention after the parent parked: the step fails as what it is rather
    than waiting for executions that no longer exist. A join signal naming
    another step: refused by name, since only forJoin() writes that column.
    
    And the claim the contract leans on — no new recovery windows — is
    now a test: a parked fan-out is an ordinary due wait, found by the
    existing waitingDue scan, so laraflow:recover delivers it with no new
    mechanism.
    
