    * Milestone 9 steps 9, 10 and 11: a failing workflow undoes what it completed
    
    A step implementing CompensatableStep is compensated when the
    execution fails terminally. Opt-in by declaring it: a workflow whose
    steps none of them do behaves exactly as before, with no policy to set
    and no rows appended — a test holds that.
    
    Each compensation is an ordinary step row, appended after the plan and
    linked to what it undoes. That decision, taken in the contract, pays
    for itself immediately: attempts, retries, timeouts, attempt history,
    laraflow:show, and the dashboard all work on compensations because
    they *are* steps. The plan is then the rows that compensate nothing,
    which is how plan validation keeps comparing like with like — held by
    a test that re-executes a compensated execution and watches the
    definition check accept it.
    
    The pass runs under the claim that ran the failing step, between the
    step's failure (durable) and the execution's (not yet) — an execution
    about to be undone has not finished failing. That meant settle() must
    stop throwing for the terminal case and return the failure instead, so
    the runner, which holds the claim and the workflow, finishes the
    execution itself. The three call sites and the wait resolver all route
    through one failTerminally().
    
    A compensation is retried under the compensated step's own policy, and
    the pass stops at the first undo whose retries are exhausted rather
    than reaching past it. Writing that found three things: the execution
    could not transition Compensating -> Retrying at all; a scheduled undo
    overwrote the execution's error with the undo's, losing the reason the
    order collapsed; and nothing resumed an interrupted pass, so the next
    claim would have re-run a plan that was finished with. The failure is
    now recorded when the pass begins, an undo's failure never overwrites
    it, and a pass under way is detected from its rows — which also means
    a scheduled undo rests as `retrying`, accurately, and the existing
    due-retry scan delivers it with no new mechanism.
    
    ExecutionStatus gains Compensating, the first case added since that
    enum promised more might be. PHPStan found the promise being broken in
    the playground's own status match, which is exactly the kind of
    consumer the warning was written for; the console and the dashboard
    render it too.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
    
    * Milestone 9 step 12: a thousand members, and the cost of deciding about them
    
    The exit criterion's number, as four tests that run in the engine
    matrix like every other: a thousand members created once and
    completed, a thousand with one failure failing the step with the
    group's shape, a thousand cancelled mid-flight with the finished half
    left alone, and — the one that matters — a measurement.
    
    The measurement found something. Resuming a parked join cost twelve
    queries at three members and thirteen at a thousand: the summary read
    every member id, which is one query per page of five hundred. Linear
    cost, hidden by a small group. It was also an unbounded payload the
    contract had already promised would be bounded, since a thousand ids
    go into a step's output column.
    
