    Two hostile reviews, concurrency leading because this milestone is
    concurrency. Both found the same shape from different directions: the
    compensating status was written durably and then stranded.
    
    A compensating execution could never be claimed again, by anyone. The
    stale *scan* was taught to return it; the claim predicate was not, and
    neither was cancellation. So a worker killed mid-pass left a row that
    recovery found, dispatched, and was refused — every run, forever — and
    that an operator could not even cancel. The domain object disagreed
    with the SQL, which is the tell: markCancelled() already permitted
    Compensating. Both predicates now accept it on a stale claim.
    
    An exhausted undo reached that same dead end without any crash, on the
    ordinary path the contract describes: the pass stopped and nothing
    rested the execution — no markFailed, no release — so it sat claimed
    and compensating with no schedule. The pass now reports three outcomes
    rather than a boolean, because "did not finish" was hiding two states
    needing opposite handling: one where the claim is already released and
    something will deliver it again, and one where nothing ever will. The
    second rests the execution on the failure that began the pass.
    
    remember() was promised inside compensate() by four documents and
    refused by the code: the context never entered the compensation's row,
    so a resumed pass had no step to scope keys to and raised, while a
    same-claim pass silently shared the failed forward step's scope. It
    enters the undo's own row now, which is also where its deadline and
    attempt number come from.
    
