    * Project audit (M0–M9), part 1: three defects in the terminal and join paths
    
    A full-project pass across security, concurrency, contract alignment,
    and code quality. Security came back clean on the surfaces that matter
    most: the reserved signal prefix is enforced case-insensitively at the
    one public entry, so the dashboard's signal action cannot reach a
    child or a join; the dashboard's start form resolves a name to a
    Workflow subclass *before* anything is built, so no arbitrary class is
    instantiated from a request; Secret keeps its value off the object and
    out of a serialized copy; and every raw SQL fragment in the package is
    parameterised or constant. The three defects below are elsewhere.
    
    **A compensated execution's failure event named the wrong class.**
    failRecorded() rests an execution on the failure recorded when the
    pass began, and wrote the real class to the column — then announced it
    with a bare WorkflowException. So a listener on
    WorkflowExecutionFailed saw every stopped or resumed saga fail as
    WorkflowException while its row said DomainException, which is the
    same defect the Milestone 9 audit fixed for error_class and missed in
    the sibling path one line down. RecordedFailure already exists for
    exactly this and reports the class it stands for; it is used here now,
    for the log line as well as the event.
    
    **A live compensation pass was refused as a finished execution.**
    docs/execution-lifecycle.md has said since the contract was written
    that a fresh claim on a `compensating` execution yields
    ExecutionAlreadyClaimedException, "as for running". The claim
    predicate learned the status in the Milestone 9 audit; the refusal
    *explanation* did not, so it fell through to
    ExecutionNotRunnableException — which public-api.md defines as "a
    terminal status" and which ExecuteWorkflowJob logs as "already
    finished (completed or cancelled)". A duplicate delivery arriving
    while a worker was undoing an execution reported the opposite of the
    truth. Both statuses now route to the claimed refusal, which names
    which of the two it is.
    
    **A second fan-out under one parent summarised no members at all.**
    memberIds() read a single page of the parent's children, oldest first,
    and filtered to the step in PHP — so for any fan-out whose members are
    all newer than one page of an earlier group's, the filter matched
    nothing and the step's output carried `members: []` beside correct
