    * Project audit (M0–M9), part 2: retention could separate a live fan-out
    
    docs/retention.md has promised since Milestone 4 that retention "can
    never separate a live pair", and gave its reasoning: a waiting parent
    is unfinished and so unprunable, and a parent waits only on an
    unfinished child. The first half holds. The second was written when a
    parent waited on exactly one child, and a fan-out broke it without
    anyone noticing — a parent parked on a join waits for the *whole
    group*, so a member that completed early is finished, and ageable,
    while its siblings are still running.
    
    The delete tier would take it. That is not a cosmetic loss: the join
    decides from childProgress(), which counts the rows that exist, so
    pruning away the members that failed turns a group that should fail
    with FanOutFailedException into one that completes with a summary. A
    saga would report success for work that did not happen, and the
    evidence would be gone.
    
    Deletion now skips any execution whose parent has not finished — a
    primary-key lookup per candidate row, asked in the scan and again
    inside the delete transaction, as every other part of the guard is.
    It is the delete tier alone: scrubbing and attempt pruning leave the
    identity and status a parent reads, so neither can mislead a join. A
    member becomes prunable once its parent finishes, under the parent's
    own bound.
    
    The document now states the guard rather than the reasoning that used
    to stand in for it, and says why the fan-out case is the one that
    needed it.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
    
    * Project audit (M0–M9), part 3: the connector host boundary, and the record
    
    Closes the audit. Two documentation corrections and the record of what
    the pass found.
    
    The host guard an application might reasonably think it has. pages()
    refuses a next-page link whose host, scheme, or port differs from the
    connection's, because Laraflow chose to follow that link out of a
    provider's response — and SECURITY.md lists "a way for a provider's
    response to redirect a request carrying credentials to another host"
    as in scope. request() has no such guard: it sends the URI the caller
    gave, and Laravel's client treats an absolute URL as a replacement for
    the base rather than a path under it, so the connection's
    Authorization header goes wherever that URL points.
    
    That stays as it is. A connector legitimately calls a signed upload
    endpoint or a redirect target off the provider's main host, and
    refusing it would break those for no gain the caller did not already
    have. What was missing is anyone saying so: a reader who found the
    pagination refusal could easily conclude the guard was general.
    docs/connectors.md now draws the line where it actually falls —
    Laraflow checks the URIs it chooses, the application checks the ones
    it builds out of a provider's response or workflow input — and
    SECURITY.md records it as a scope boundary rather than leaving it to
    be reported as a finding.
    
    Also: WorkflowCompensationStarted now documents what its
    WorkflowCompensated counterpart already did, that it fires once per
    pass attempt rather than once per saga, with `steps` counting what is
    left rather than what the first pass faced. The behaviour was right
    and only one of the pair said so.
    
    The roadmap and changelog carry the audit's record: four code defects
    fixed across the three parts, the security surfaces that came back
    clean, and the boundary left deliberately where it was.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
    
    * Fix the engine and floor CI cells, reproduced locally against every one
    
    Four failures, reproduced in containers matching each cell — MySQL 8.0,
    MariaDB 10.6, PostgreSQL 13 and SQL Server 2019, on PHP 8.2 with
    Laravel 12 at the dependency floor and PHP 8.4 with Laravel 13 on the
    lock — rather than guessed at from the matrix.
    
    **SQL Server: a compensation row could not be read back.** pdo_sqlsrv
    returns every column as a string, which is why every model casts its
    foreign keys to integer and says so in a comment. Milestone 9 added
    `compensates_step_execution_id` and did not add its cast, so
    StepExecution's `?int` constructor was handed a string and fatalled —
    on that one engine, in five compensation tests and the saga inspection
    suite. The cast is there now.
    
    The rule that should have caught it exists and could not: the contract
    test holding every id to `int` skips Eloquent models, "which cast
    them", and nothing checked that they do. A companion test now reads
    the live schema and holds every `*_id` column on every model to an
    integer cast, so the next column cannot be missed either. Removing the
    cast again turns it red.
    
    **MySQL and MariaDB: the retention guard could not be a subquery.**
    Mine, from the audit two commits back. Those engines refuse to name a
    DELETE's own target table inside a subquery (error 1093), and the
    live-parent guard did exactly that — taking out fifteen retention
    tests and nine prune-command ones. The guard resolves to a list of ids
    by two primary-key reads before the DELETE now, which is the
    read-then-write shape `stillEligible()` already uses. The scan keeps
    the subquery: it is a SELECT, which every engine allows.
    
    **MySQL: the fan-out summary's keys came back in another order.** The
    JSON column type sorts object keys by length, so a summary written
    total/completed/members reads back total/members/completed, and
    assertSame compares order. The shape is the claim and the key order is
    the engine's business, so the empty-group test uses assertEquals, as
    the rest of the file already does for values read out of JSON.
    
    **PHP 8.2 at the floor: static analysis failed on `modify()`.**
    DateTimeImmutable::modify() returns `DateTimeImmutable|false` on 8.2
    (it throws from 8.3), so passing its result to a `?DateTimeImmutable`
    parameter is an error on the oldest supported PHP — which only the
    prefer-lowest cell runs analysis on. Compensator computed an undo's
    deadline that way while the runner, four hundred lines off, computed
    the same deadline with `add(new DateInterval(...))` precisely because
    of this; RetentionPolicy::cutoff() has a comment explaining it. One
    `StepPolicy::deadlineFrom()` now answers for both callers, since a
    compensation's deadline and a forward step's were always the same
    question.
    
    Also, found while ruling it out as the cause: SecretExposureTest built
    `print_r($frame['args'], true)` over argument graphs that reach the
    container and the HTTP client, allocating hundreds of megabytes and
    killing the suite wherever zend.exception_ignore_args is off — the
    stock PHP default, and not what php.ini-production and so CI use. The
    assertion had therefore never run. It walks the graph now under this
    file's own scoping rule, and the one test that wrote a credential into
    its own request URI uses a separate sentinel: finding the caller's
    input in the caller's object is not a leak, and conflating the two was
    the only thing the walk had to say.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
    
