    * Milestone 8 step 8: the versioning and idempotency contracts, decided before built
    
    Two documents fix the semantics of Phase B's capabilities before any
    code exists, as docs/advanced-workflows.md did for Milestone 7. The
    roadmap has required persistence, versioning, recovery, and
    observability semantics ahead of implementation since Milestone 0.
    
    docs/workflow-versioning.md. A workflow declares version(), recorded
    as plan_version on the execution, and planMigrations() keyed by the
    version each hop migrates from. A change with no declared migration
    still fails as a definition mismatch — the refusal stays the default
    and silence means refuse. The decision everything rests on: a
    migration may rewrite only the undecided tail of a plan, because a
    pending step row has no output, no attempt history, and no context
    contribution, so it can be renumbered or removed without rewriting
    history, while every other row is the record of something that
    happened. Migrations run lazily under the existing claim in one
    transaction before the first step write, so a faulting one is bounded
    by the fruitless-claims counter rather than needing a new mechanism,
    and the migrated plan still faces the ordinary definition check: the
    migration proposes, the definition judges.
    
    docs/idempotency.md. $context->remember(key, work) runs the work at
    most once per execution across every retry, recovery, and worker. The
    decision everything rests on: the marker commits before the work runs,
    so a crash leaves positive evidence that the work started, and the
    store can tell "never began" from "began and never came back". The
    latter is raised as IdempotentOutcomeUncertainException rather than
    silently re-run or silently skipped — one is how a card is charged
    twice, the other is how it is never charged — with an optional
    reconcile callback for providers that can be asked what happened. A
    thrown exception is a known outcome and releases the record; a dead
    process is not, and leaves it. An exception that means "unknown"
    despite being thrown says so by implementing IndicatesUncertainOutcome,
    mirroring SuggestsRetryDelay. A unique key per execution is what stops
    two live workers both performing the side effect, enforced by the
    database rather than by timing.
    
    Idempotency gets its own document rather than a section in
    execution-lifecycle.md, which is already the densest contract at 191
    lines; it is linked from there when step 10 lands. Neither document
    names a config key that does not exist yet, which is the rule the
