    * Milestone 8 step 9: a deploy that changes a workflow no longer strands it
    
    Workflow versioning, implemented against docs/workflow-versioning.md.
    A workflow declares version() — default 1, recorded as plan_version on
    every execution it creates — and planMigrations(), callables keyed by
    the version each migrates from, receiving a PlanDraft and editing the
    step list by class name. A claimed execution that is behind runs the
    declared hops in turn, has its plan rewritten, and carries on. A
    workflow that changes steps() without declaring a version and a
    migration still fails as a definition mismatch: the refusal is the
    default and silence means refuse.
    
    The rule the whole mechanism rests on is that a migration may rewrite
    only the undecided tail of a plan. A pending step row has no output,
    no attempt history, and no context contribution, so it can be
    renumbered, removed, or replaced without rewriting history; every
    other row is the record of something that happened. The tail is
    rewritten by deleting and reinserting it, because (execution,
    position) is unique and an in-place renumber collides with itself
    halfway through on every engine — safe for exactly the same reason.
    Reaching further back is refused by name, with the status that froze
    the step.
    
    Migrations run lazily, under the existing claim, in one claim-guarded
    transaction, before the first step write, so a faulting one is bounded
    by the fruitless-claims counter that already bounds every fault in
    that position; the rewrite deliberately does not clear that counter,
    because migrating is not reaching a step. The migrated plan then faces
    the ordinary definition check unchanged: the migration proposes,
    steps() judges. A record ahead of the code is never carried backwards.
    
    Four of the contract's guarantees were checked by reintroducing their
    bug: the frozen prefix, the rollback refusal, the plan check after a
    migration, and the claim counter. The last one was not caught by the
    tests as first written — the case it needs is a migration that commits
