    * Milestone 8 step 10: a side effect runs once, and a crash inside the window is told, not guessed
    
    The idempotency store, implemented against docs/idempotency.md.
    $context->remember('charge', fn () => ...) runs the work at most once
    per execution across every retry, recovery, and worker, and replays
    the stored value thereafter.
    
    The ordering the design rests on: the marker commits before the work
    runs, the value after. A worker that dies between them leaves a
    pending record — positive evidence the work began, none that it
    finished — and the next attempt is raised IdempotentOutcomeUncertain-
    Exception rather than silently re-running or silently skipping. One
    is how a customer is charged twice; the other is how they are never
    charged. A reconciler, if given, is asked first: a non-null answer is
    the outcome, null asserts the side effect never landed and the work
    runs again. A thrown exception is a known outcome and the record is
    marked failed, unless the exception implements IndicatesUncertain-
    Outcome — the counterpart of SuggestsRetryDelay — which leaves it
    pending for the timeout-after-sending case.
    
    (execution, key) is unique in the database, so two live workers in
    the same step cannot both perform the side effect: one insert wins and
    the other finds a pending record. The claim check cannot help here;
    it runs after the side effect. Keys are scoped to the step, validated,
    and capped per execution; values obey the serialization contract; the
    store follows payload through retention, with scrub never touching a
    failed execution's records for the reason it never touches its input
    and context; and laraflow:show lists remembered work without ever
    printing a value.
    
    Twenty-two tests, including the worker dying at the value write, the
    second worker losing the unique-key race, a reconciler answering each
    way, and the per-execution cap releasing the marker it refused. Four
    guarantees were checked by reintroducing their bug — a silent re-run on
    an unknown outcome, every exception treated as known, a begin() that
    pretends it won the race, and a cap that keeps the refused marker —
    and every one was caught.
    
    The context holds the resolver as a nullable collaborator, deliberately:
    it is a value object the runner builds, and one built by hand in a test
    has no execution behind it. remember() on such a context raises rather
    than running the work, which is the behaviour the store exists to end.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
    
    * Milestone 8 step 10a: the Phase B audit — three hostile reviews, two criticals, the rest made right
    
    Three parallel reviews of Phase B — concurrency and crash windows,
    code and test quality, claim-by-claim contract alignment — produced
    54 findings. Two were critical, two were defects, and all four sat in
    the idempotency store and the plan migrator shipped two commits ago.
    
    The store could recurse until the worker died on MySQL, MariaDB, and
    SQL Server. Those engines compare strings case-insensitively by
    default, so a second key differing from a recorded one only in case —
    or, on MariaDB, in trailing whitespace — violated the unique index,
    was read back with a byte-exact comparison that found nothing, and
    was inserted again, forever. Confirmed with raw SQL against MySQL 8.
    Such a key is now refused by name, keys may not begin or end with
    whitespace, and the read-back is bounded.
    
    On PostgreSQL a replay of a recorded key inside any transaction failed
    outright: a failed statement aborts the transaction there, so the
    marker insert's unique violation left the read-back unable to run — in
    the test suite, which runs inside one, and for any application calling
    remember() inside its own. The insert now runs in its own savepoint.
    This one is proved by CI's PostgreSQL job, not locally; the local PHP
    has only SQLite.
    
    release() deleted whatever record was there, so two attempts that both
    found a failed record could both begin again — the second deleting the
    first's live pending marker — and both run the work the unique key
    exists to protect. It now removes only a failed record and reports
    whether it did; settle() and fail() likewise touch only a pending one.
    
    The plan migrator committed a wrong migration, announced it, and only
    then let the definition refuse it, leaving a plan no code describes
    and plan_version saying the migration had happened. The judge rules
    before the write now: nothing is committed, the author fixes the
    closure, the next claim tries again.
    
    Taken from the reviews besides: the store carries the caller's key as
    written, unique with its step position, so the format is known in one
    place and a key's length limit no longer shrinks as execution ids grow
    digits; the table's redundant second index is gone; PlanDraft refuses
    an operation that would repeat a class, names the decided row that
    froze a pending one, journals its operations for the migration log,
    and offers of() for a migration's own tests; a published
    IdempotencyStoreContract holds a custom store to the guarantees — the
    natural Eloquent idiom for begin() would report every first call as
    uncertain, and that test is what catches it; and the README stopped
    listing workflow versioning as a thing Laraflow does not do.
    
    Every fix testable on SQLite was checked by reintroducing its bug.
    
