    Milestone 7: Advanced Workflows and v0.2.0 Stable Release Readiness (#4)
    
    * Milestone 7 step 1: every step-row write is made under the claim
    
    saveStep() wrote the step row by id, so a worker stalled past
    stale_after whose execution had been reclaimed could still mark a step
    the new owner was running as running or failed, or append a stale
    attempt row, before its first checkpoint or release stopped it with
    ClaimLostException. The executions row was never corrupted this way,
    but step status and attempt history could be — the first of the two
    claim-discipline limits recorded at the Milestone 6 audit.
    
    saveStep() now takes the claimed WorkflowExecution and verifies the
    claim in the same transaction as the step and attempt writes, touching
    the executions row with the same guarded, version-bumping UPDATE that
    checkpoint() and release() already use; on loss the whole transaction
    rolls back. OutcomeRecorder lets ClaimLostException propagate instead
    of logging it as a storage failure, so a reclaimed worker leaves by the
    quiet path the queue job already handles.
    
    Breaking for custom ExecutionRepository implementations, recorded in
    the changelog and the public API document. The lifecycle contract's
    claim-verification paragraph now covers every write, and the known
    limit is retired. Regression tests at the repository level (a reclaimed
    worker's failure write and attempt row roll back together; the guard
    touches nothing but the version) and at the runner level (a takeover
    mid-step leaves the step row exactly as the new owner found it, with
    no stale history and no storage-failure log).
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
    
    * Milestone 7 step 2: a claim that reaches no step is counted, and bounded
    
    Recovery's abandonment bound read the highest step attempt count, which
    is durable and needed no extra state — but a worker that faults between
    claiming and its first step write never moves it. A listener throwing on
    WorkflowExecutionStarted, or a workflow whose steps no longer match the
    persisted plan, left the execution running on an aging claim with
    attempts at zero, and laraflow:recover re-dispatched it on every run
    forever, each run occupying a claim for stale_after. The second of the
    two claim-discipline limits recorded at the Milestone 6 audit.
    
    The executions table gains fruitless_claims, the first migration since
    the schema freeze: additive, default 0, reversible, safe under live
    workers. claim() increments it; the first step write under the claim
    zeroes it inside the guarded UPDATE step 1 added, so the ordinary path
    pays no extra query. Recovery holds it to the same bound as step
    attempts, and the report names which measure tripped. laraflow:show
    displays the count once an execution has been claimed more than once
    without progress.
    
    Tests: the repository counts and resets; the scan abandons at the bound
    and not below it, and a step write clears the run; an end-to-end
    definition mismatch is re-dispatched twice and abandoned on the third
    scan with no further claim taken; and UpgradePathTest now exercises the
    real migration — rolled back one step on a populated database, the
    install and its rows survive, and reapplied, the predating row reads 0
    and takes part in the new behaviour. The freeze expectation and the
    migration column list move with it, in the same commit, as the freeze
    test demands.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
    
