    * Milestone 7 step 3: the dispatched event waits for the commit the push waits for
    
    With after_commit on and dispatch() called inside an application
    transaction, the job push was correctly deferred to the commit — but
    WorkflowExecutionDispatched was emitted when dispatch() returned,
    before it. A listener reading the database on the event could find
    nothing, and a rolled-back transaction had announced an execution that
    never existed. Every other lifecycle event fired after its durable
    write; this one was documented as the exception.
    
    The dispatcher now registers the event with the same transaction
    manager Laravel's queue defers the push through, after the push's own
    callback: on commit it fires once the job is on the queue, on rollback
    it never fires, and outside a transaction — or with after_commit off —
    the manager runs it at once, as before. The provider hands the manager
    in; WorkflowDispatcher gains a trailing optional constructor argument.
    
    Three tests: the event is absent inside the transaction and present
    after it; a rolled-back dispatch leaves no execution and no event; and
    with after_commit off the event fires inside the transaction. The
    known limit is retired from the queue document and the roadmap.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
    
    * Milestone 7 step 4: the sent event carries the connector's verdict
    
    ConnectorRequestSent.successful reflected HTTP status alone: the event
    was emitted by the observing transport, the outermost pipeline layer,
    and the connector's error mapper ran after the pipeline returned. A
    provider that rejects a call inside a 200 body — Slack's ok: false —
    was announced as successful while the caller's response said failed.
    The last event-ordering limit recorded at the Milestone 6 audit.
    
    The mapper now runs inside the pipeline: a MappingTransport sits
    between the retry layer and the observing layer, so only the response
    that survives transport retries is mapped, and the event announces the
    response the caller actually gets. HttpConnector hands the pipeline a
    closure built around the request as the caller made it, so the mapper
    still never sees anything the authenticator added, and the request the
    transports carry stays marked sensitive.
    
    TransportPipeline::through() gains a trailing optional mapping closure;
    the connectors and events documents drop the limit and state the new
    contract. The regression test stages Slack's rejected 200 and asserts
    the event reports it unsuccessful while carrying no error detail —
    identifiers and measurements only, as before.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
    
