    * Milestone 7 step 5: one header-merge rule, matched the way the wire reads
    
    Header names merged case-sensitively at every layer: a connection
    configuring `accept` under a connector class declaring `Accept` sent
    both headers and left the provider to pick one, and a request's
    `content-type` joined the options' `Content-Type` the same way. The
    last of the five hardening items recorded at the Milestone 6 audit.
    
    All four merge points — class headers under connection headers in
    forConnection(), the implicit API-version header in withHeaders(), the
    request-over-options merge in the transport, and the request's own
    withHeaders() — now go through Integration\Http\Headers::merge(), which
    matches names case-insensitively per RFC 9110 §5.1. The overriding
    layer wins the value and the spelling; an overridden header keeps its
    position; a second spelling of the same name within one layer collapses
    to the later one rather than reappearing beside its replacement.
    
    Tests at each layer: the connection override whatever its casing, the
    implicit version header refusing to join an explicitly pinned one, the
    transport sending exactly one differently-spelled Accept, and the
    request replacing its own If-None-Match. Each fails under a plain
    array_merge. The connectors document's sharp-edge paragraph shrinks to
    the one edge that remains (the unmapped-304 throw).
    
