    * Milestone 7 step 6: the advanced-workflows contract, decided before built
    
    docs/advanced-workflows.md fixes the semantics for the milestone's
    three capabilities before any code exists: conditional steps, delays
    and wait states with signals, and sub-workflow composition. The
    roadmap has required persistence, versioning, recovery, and
    observability semantics ahead of implementation since Milestone 0;
    this document is that requirement discharged in one place, marked as a
    design contract until each section's step lands its enforcing tests.
    
    The decisions that shape everything downstream: a wait is the step's
    outcome boundary (resume completes the step, handle() never resumes
    mid-body); signal payloads are the waiting step's output and land in
    the context under the signal's name; wait timeouts are ordinary step
    failures under the step's own retry policy; composition is the wait
    mechanism plus two linkage columns, with a parent-step retry resuming
    the existing child rather than spawning a sibling; conditions are pure
    functions of the context and a skip is durable, attempt-free, and
    context-neutral; and next_retry_at stays the one "when can anything
    next happen" instant, leaving three genuinely new columns across all
    three features.
    
    The roadmap records the scope decision — fan-out, saga, and tenant
    throttling stay deferred. The document avoids naming commands and
    config keys that do not exist yet, so the documentation contract tests
    hold it to the same standard as the shipped contracts.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
    
    * Milestone 7 step 7: a condition decides once, and the record is the decision
    
    A step may implement ConditionalStep — shouldRun(context), consulted at
    the step's turn, after the cancellation check and before anything is
    marked running. False durably marks the step skipped in a claim-guarded
    write: no attempt counted, no history row, no output, context untouched,
    completed_at holding the instant the decision was recorded. Recovery
    replays the record; the regression test flips an impure condition after
    the skip persists and proves it gets no second vote. A throwing
    condition fails the step exactly like a step class that cannot be
    built, and a step past pending never consults its condition — it
    already answered true, and a pure condition over an unchanged context
    cannot change its mind.
    
    New WorkflowStepSkipped event after the durable write. StepStatus gains
    Skipped and stepProgress() a skipped count — both breaking as recorded
    in the changelog — and laraflow:list counts completed-plus-skipped as
    done so a finished execution with skips does not read as stuck. The
    semantics are docs/advanced-workflows.md section one, now enforced.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
    
    * Milestone 7 step 8: a wait is the step's outcome boundary
    
    A step may return a Wait from handle() instead of an output: timed
    (until an instant) or on a named signal, optionally deadlined. The park
    checkpoints everything the step did first — step row waiting, signal
    name, resume instant or deadline, and the context, in one claim-guarded
    transaction — then the execution parks with its claim released and
    next_retry_at holding the next instant anything can happen; null means
    an indefinite signal wait only its signal can wake. Resuming completes
    the step: the signal payload (or null) becomes its output and lands in
    the context under the signal's name, and handle() never resumes
    mid-body — one run of the body is one attempt row, closed when the
    wait resolves, spanning run and park alike.
    
    Signals: WorkflowRunner::signal() bounds the payload, refuses empty and
    reserved names, and records through the new recordSignal() repository
    primitive — payload onto the waiting step row and due-now onto the
    execution in one transaction, refused with SignalRefusedException when
    nothing is parked on that name. Recording is not delivery: the delayed
    queue job covers known instants, and laraflow:recover gains due-waiting
    executions as a fourth candidate kind (wait_due), exempt from the
    abandonment bound like due retries. A deadline passing fails the step
    with WaitTimedOutException under its own retry policy — a retried
    approval step re-posts its request, by design. Cancelling a waiting
    execution is immediate; a crash between park and release heals by
    parking again, proven by test; a claim refused by an unexpired wait
    explains itself with ExecutionStillWaitingException, saying
    "indefinitely" when only a signal can wake it.
    
    Schema: one additive migration, wait_signal on step rows — resume and
    deadline reuse next_retry_at and timeout_at, so the existing indexes
    and scan shapes carry over. markCompleted() keeps timeout_at and
    next_retry_at as the attempt's record, as the timeout tests already
    pinned. Nine tests cover park, early-claim refusal, resume, payload
    round-trip, refusals, timeout, retry-and-repost, cancellation, and the
    crash heal. Contract: docs/advanced-workflows.md, section three.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
    
    * Milestone 7 step 9: a sub-workflow is a wait, two columns, and an outcome
    
    A step extending SubWorkflowStep runs another workflow as a child
    execution. The child is created once — linked by parent_execution_id
    (indexed) and parent_step_execution_id, plain columns rather than
    foreign keys so retention may prune a long-finished parent while the
    child's audit record lives on — inherits the parent's correlation and
    tenant identifiers, and is handed to the new ChildDelivery seam, whose
    default binding is Laraflow's queue dispatcher; the sync driver runs
    the child in-process. The parent parks on the reserved child signal;
    the child's final write records its identity and status (never its
    context) on the parent and asks delivery to run it. A completed child
    is the step's output; a failed or cancelled one fails the step with
    ChildWorkflowFailedException under the step's own retry policy — and a
    retry resumes the same child from its checkpoints rather than spawning
    a sibling, proven by test.
    
    The park heartbeats on stale_after instead of waiting indefinitely:
    when claimed, the parent consults the child's record directly, so a
    signal lost to the park race becomes the outcome, a lost dispatch is
    re-delivered under the claim's protection, and a pruned child surfaces
    as missing rather than hanging. Cancelling a parent cascades to
    unfinished children, recursively and visibly; a cancelled child cannot
    be resumed and says so on every retry. Nesting is bounded at creation
    by laraflow.workflows.max_depth, a walk the limit itself bounds.
    
    ExecutionFilter gains parentId, ExecutionOptions the linkage ids,
    laraflow:show names a child's parent, and the third post-freeze
    migration rides the upgrade harness with the other two. The runner's
    no-queue principle holds: ChildDelivery is the abstraction, resolved
    lazily to break the runner–dispatcher cycle. Eight tests cover linkage
    and inheritance, outcome round-trip, same-child retry, the heartbeat,
    the depth bound, both cancellation directions, and the parent filter.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
    
    * Milestone 7 step 9a: the Phase B audit — three hostile reviews, every defect fixed
    
    Three parallel reviews over steps 6-9 (concurrency, code quality,
    contract alignment) produced 48 findings. The defects, each now fixed
    with a regression test:
    
    The worst were in composition's long paths. Healthy heartbeats counted
    toward abandonment — nothing on the re-park path reset fruitless_claims,
    so a parent waiting on a slow child was one crash away from recovery
    refusing it forever; an orderly release now zeroes the counter. A stale
    child-failure payload survived the retry re-park on the parent step's
    output and read as the child's current outcome at the next heartbeat,
    charging one failure twice; markWaiting clears the output and a failure
    predating the current attempt is resumed, not re-charged. The heartbeat
    also re-delivered any unfinished child, including a crash-looping one
    recovery had abandoned — it now delivers only lost dispatches and
    pre-attempt failures, leaving the rest to the machinery that owns their
    bounds.
    
    Conditional steps had two: a durable skip could be recorded against a
    context missing a failed step's contribution under Continue (no longer
    persisted — the condition votes again on re-execution), and after a
    throwing condition the re-executed step ran unconditionally (the
    condition is now consulted at the start of every attempt).
    
    Cancellation had two: a request landing mid-step could park
    indefinitely (the park is a boundary and honours it now) and a
    cooperatively-honoured cancellation never cascaded, orphaning a child
    created mid-race (it cascades, staged and proven by test). Deliveries
    racing a park failed jobs and recovery runs through the uncaught
    ExecutionStillWaitingException — now absorbed as information, like the
    races before it. recordSignal refused idempotent duplicates on engines
    counting changed rows; the waiting check is a read now, and a duplicate
    overwrites. Plus the small ones: filter copies dropping parentId (and
    its constructor position un-broken), the 500-child lookup page, the
    pruned-ancestor depth walk, summary's blindness to waiting work,
    show's dishonest child-wait line, and --skip-retries' silence about
    waits.
    
    The reviews also surfaced 26 stale statements across every contract
    document — state machines, the claim predicate, the recovery kinds,
    event tables, retention's status enumeration, and a README still
    claiming these features do not exist. All corrected; the runner's three
    duplicated completion blocks collapsed into one completeStep(), the
    stringly-typed wait resolutions became WaitResolution, and the recovery
    path that was shipped untested (wait_due) is tested now.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
    
