    * Milestone 7 steps 11–12: the closing audit — seven reviews, 190 findings, the defects fixed
    
    Seven parallel hostile reviews over the whole project — workflow-core
    concurrency, the connector and secrets layer, documentation alignment,
    code standards, test-suite quality, schema and query performance, and a
    branch security review — produced 190 findings. Every claim was
    verified against the code before anything was touched; three were
    dismissed on verification (PostgreSQL binds whole seconds, so the
    rounding premise was false), the design findings not worth the churn
    before 1.0 are recorded under Deferred in the roadmap, and the rest are
    fixed here, each with a regression test.
    
    The foundations had real defects that no milestone had exercised. A
    step returning a scalar crashed the checkpoint with a TypeError and
    left the execution claimed — Step::handle(): mixed, a repository typed
    ?array, since Milestone 1. The reserved signal prefix was enforced
    byte-wise in PHP while the waiting-step match ran in a database that
    compares case-insensitively on three of five engines, so a
    differently-cased name could forge a sub-workflow's completion; the
    match is now a locked read compared byte for byte. A checkpoint failing
    after the handle said "completed" drove every failure path into an
    illegal transition that masked the real error. Under Continue, a step
    that failed terminally was re-run on every later resume, and a failed
    step's context writes leaked into the next checkpoint. Abandoned
    executions could starve the recovery scans and could not be cancelled.
    Two retention scans cost the size of history rather than the backlog,
    and a finished-window filter with no status was a full table scan
    through the public API — a fourth post-freeze migration and one SQL
    predicate fix both. Anonymous exception class names broke the failure
    write on PostgreSQL.
    
    The suite itself had holes the reviews named: a backwards
    assertLessThanOrEqual on the Retry-After cap, a "no cascades" test
    whose PRAGMA was a documented no-op inside a transaction, a pagination
    loop whose second case never ran, a documented-class check satisfied by
    any basename in the prose, and a schema freeze that pinned names but
    not shapes. Each is fixed; the freeze now pins the migration files'
    hashes. Twenty-two new tests, 949 in all.
    
    Documentation caught up everywhere the reviews found it lagging: the
    recovery doc's abandoned-budget and cancellation rules, upgrading's
    "only touches interrupted work", the sync-driver default claim (Laravel
    12 and 13 default to database), README's requirements and events, the
    repository contract's own docblocks, retention's status enumeration and
    the archive recipe's off-by-one, SECURITY.md's two sinks beneath
    Laraflow, and the connectors document's worst-case transport bound.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
    
    * Milestone 7 step 13: the closing record, 0.2.0 prepared, and the road to 1.0
    
    The changelog's Unreleased section becomes 0.2.0 — breaking changes on
