    The three shipped migrations are now immutable. A later schema change
    arrives as a new dated migration, and an installed application reaches
    it with php artisan migrate — never by being told to drop its tables.
    
    A policy nobody checks is a wish, so SchemaFreezeTest pins the exact
    columns, indexes, and table list the shipped migrations produce, and
    fails with the reason and the supported alternative when they move. I
    verified it by slipping a column into a frozen migration and watching
    it fail by name. UpgradePathTest is the other half: it applies a new
    dated migration to an already-populated database and asserts the rows
    survive, that the added column reads as null on rows that predate it,
    that rolling the migration back leaves the frozen tables and their
    data intact, and that re-running migrate with nothing to do is a
    no-op. It is also the harness the first real migration will use.
    
    docs/upgrading.md states the contract that follows: the ordinary
    upgrade, upgrading with a schema change while workers run, checking
    against a copy first, rolling back schema and code in that order,
    recovering interrupted work, and what to do when it goes wrong. The
    instruction to reinstall the tables is gone from the README,
    CONTRIBUTING, and compatibility; public-api and
