    and an example. A scheduled prune that silently does nothing is
    indistinguishable from one that is working, right up until someone
    looks at the size of the attempt table — so an unconfigured run is
    reported as the misconfiguration it is rather than as a successful
    no-op. That holds for --dry-run too: there is nothing to predict.
    
