    Milestone 4 step 10: the retention engine
    
    Three tiers over finished work, every bound null by default. Retention
    is irreversible, and a package that quietly began deleting an
    application's execution history on upgrade would be indefensible, so
    Laraflow removes nothing until it is told what to keep — and even then
    only when laraflow:prune runs (step 11).
    
    Scrub clears input, context, and step outputs of completed and
    cancelled executions, keeping the record as an audit trail of what ran
    and when. It never touches failed executions: re-executing one is the
    documented recovery path and needs exactly those values, so scrubbing
    would leave a record that still advertises itself as recoverable and
    is not — a failure that would surface much later as a workflow running
    against an empty context, and be very hard to trace back to a
    retention setting.
    
    Prune attempts deletes the append-only history, which is the table
    that actually grows without bound under retry policies and the reason
    this milestone exists. It does cover failed executions, and the
    asymmetry with scrubbing is deliberate: the step row keeps the latest
    attempt's status, error, and counter, and the retry budget reads that
    counter rather than this table, so only older diagnostics are lost.
    
    Delete removes completed and cancelled executions outright. Failed
    ones answer to a separate delete_failed_after_days, because discarding
    work that can still be recovered should be a decision an operator took
    rather than a side effect of configuring general retention.
    
    Bounds count from finished_at, so a long-running execution is never
    pruned for having started long ago, and only finished work is eligible
    at all: pending, running, and retrying executions are invisible to
    every scan and every write. A retry waiting out its backoff is future
    work, not old work.
    
    Three properties the design turns on, each tested:
    
    Every write re-applies the status and finished_at guard in the same
    statement. A failed execution is re-executable, so retention is always
    racing a possible recovery; claiming sets status = running and clears
    finished_at, so an execution claimed between scan and write matches
    zero rows and is silently spared — along with its steps and attempts,
    because the orphan sweep removes children of rows that actually went,
    not of the ids we asked about.
    
    Every tier is self-limiting, or a scheduled run would spend its limit
    rescanning work already done and never reach anything newer. Delete
    removes the row; scrub requires payloads_scrubbed_at to be null; and
    attempt pruning requires that attempt rows still exist, served by the
    index the attempts table has carried since Milestone 3 — which is why
    the scans take a tier rather than a generic filter, and why no new
    schema was needed.
    
    A dry run predicts the run that follows it. The first draft did not:
    it deleted nothing, so the gentler tiers still saw rows a real run
    would have destroyed and over-reported. Would-be-deleted ids are now
    carried forward, and a test asserts the two reports agree field by
    field. A dry run whose numbers differ from the real run fails at the
    only thing it exists to do.
    
    Deletion leaves no orphaned steps or attempts whether or not the
    engine enforces the declared cascade — SQLite does so only when the
    connection asks, and a test runs the whole path with PRAGMA
    foreign_keys = OFF.
    
    BREAKING: ExecutionRepository gained retentionCandidates(),
    scrubPayloads(), pruneAttempts(), and deleteExecutions(). Custom
    implementations must add them and honour the three obligations above.
