    Milestone 4 step 9: a payload size policy at the durable boundaries
    
    laraflow.execution.max_payload_bytes bounds how large one durable
    payload may be — the workflow input, a step's output, or the context
    snapshot — measured as the JSON that actually reaches the column
    rather than the in-memory footprint, which bears no fixed relation to
    it. Null by default; the only limit stays the database's unless an
    application asks for one. This is the item docs/execution-lifecycle.md
    deferred to this milestone.
    
    Setting a limit does not turn a success into a failure. An oversized
    payload already fails — as a driver error about a column type or a
    packet size, raised from inside the checkpoint transaction, naming
    nothing an operator can act on. The limit moves that failure earlier
    and gives it a name: PayloadTooLargeException reporting the path, the
    actual size, and the configured limit.
    
    Enforced at exactly the three points the type contract already
    guards, because they are the same boundaries: input at start(), where
    a rejection leaves no execution and no step plan behind, and output
    and context after handle() returns but before the checkpoint, so
    nothing oversized reaches a column. Each is measured independently,
    since each is written to its own. A context that grows past the limit
    fails the step that grew it while earlier checkpoints stand, and
    re-execution still skips them.
    
    The exception joins StepPolicy::NEVER_RETRY. A step that produced an
    oversized output will produce the same one next time, so a retry
    budget could only fill the schedule with work that cannot succeed —
    proven by a test giving the failing step five attempts and asserting
    it ran once.
    
    DurableValue gains check(), which applies the type contract and then
    the size, in that order: an unrepresentable value is a serialization
    fault whatever its size and must be reported as one. Size measurement
    substitutes invalid UTF-8 rather than failing, because the method
    measures and does not validate — letting the encoder return false
    there would silently skip the limit on exactly the payloads most
    likely to be malformed.
    
    A blank LARAFLOW_MAX_PAYLOAD_BYTES means "no limit", consistent with
    every other key; a configured limit below 1 is rejected at
    construction rather than silently rejecting every payload including
    an empty one.
    
