    Milestone 4 step 8: structured logging context and an optional lifecycle log
    
    While a step runs, Laravel's log context carries the execution's
    identity: laraflow.execution_id, workflow, step, position, attempt,
    and — when supplied — correlation_id and tenant_id. The point is that
    no Laraflow API is involved: an application's own Log::info() from
    inside a step picks them up, and because Laravel dehydrates the
    context into queue payloads, so does any job that step dispatches. One
    correlation id follows the work across processes.
    
    Keys are namespaced because the context belongs to the application; a
    package writing a bare correlation_id would silently clobber whatever
    was already there. Absent identifiers are omitted rather than written
    as null, which would put a meaningless key in every record. The
    context is restored in a finally, so a throw, a scheduled retry, or a
    cancellation all leave it exactly as found and an in-process run
    cannot leak its execution id into the surrounding request. Nested
    executions — a step synchronously running another workflow — push and
    pop frames, restoring the outer execution's keys rather than deleting
    them, so the remainder of the outer run is still attributed correctly.
    Even a laraflow.* key the application set itself is put back, because
    we did not create it.
    
    execute() is split: it claims, enters the context, and delegates to
    runClaimed() inside a try/finally. The alternative was indenting the
    entire run by one level, which would have buried the change.
    
    The playground found a real ordering defect before this shipped.
    WorkflowStepStarted was announced before the step keys were set, so
    its record — and any listener logging during it — carried the previous
    step's name, or none at all on the first step. The context now
    narrows before the announcement, with a test asserting a listener on
    StepStarted sees its own step.
    
    laraflow.logging.lifecycle (off) adds one log record per event, for
    applications that want an execution trail without writing listeners.
    It is a wildcard listener on Laraflow\Events\*, so an event class
    added later is logged without touching the logger; terminal failures
    log at error and failed attempts at warning, matching the guidance to
    alert on WorkflowExecutionFailed rather than every attempt. The
    record's context is the event's public properties, which is safe by
    construction — events carry identifiers and metadata only — and a test
    runs a workflow whose step copies a secret into its output and
    asserts the secret appears nowhere in the log.
    
    illuminate/log is now declared; it was already present transitively.
    LoggingContext degrades to a no-op when no context repository is
    bound, so a non-Laravel container can still resolve the runner.
    
