    the execution's *first* start to this finish. That is end-to-end
    latency, not compute time: started_at is preserved across
    re-executions, so an execution that failed and was recovered an hour
    later reports the hour. This is deliberate and documented in three
    places — it is what an SLA measures and what "how long did the customer
    wait" means, and per-attempt compute time is already on the step events
    and in the attempt history. Null when the execution never started, such
    as a pending execution cancelled outright; zero stays a measured zero.
    
