    Milestone 4 step 6: queue lag, measured from availability rather than dispatch
    
    Every dispatch now records when the execution was handed to the queue
    and when the queue was asked to make it runnable. Those are the same
    instant for an ordinary push and differ for a delayed retry, where
    available_at is the retry's due time — and the whole design rests on
    that distinction. A retry held back by its own two-minute backoff has
    not been waiting for a worker for two minutes; counting it would make
    every backoff look like a slow queue. Lag is therefore claim time minus
    available_at, and the fast-path redelivery ExecuteWorkflowJob queues
    for next_retry_at passes that due time through, which the test pins.
    
    WorkflowExecutionStarted carries the result as queueLagSeconds. It is
    null — not zero — when the execution was never dispatched or was
    claimed ahead of availability by an operator running a scheduled retry
    early: in neither case did it wait for a worker, and zero would be a
    measurement where there was none. For an execution recovered after a
    lost dispatch, the wait is measured from the re-dispatch recovery made,
    which is the delivery actually claimed; recovered in-process instead,
    it is how long the work sat undelivered, which is the number an
    operator wants.
    
    The dispatcher records the dispatch before pushing the job. On the sync
    driver the job runs inside dispatch(), and a claim that happens before
    the record exists cannot measure its own wait — a test runs the sync
    path and asserts a lag of exactly zero. Each dispatch overwrites the
    last so the columns always describe the most recent delivery, and the
    write is deliberately not claim-guarded: delivery metadata is not
    execution state, and recovery must be able to record a re-dispatch of
    a running execution whose dead worker's claim is still on the row.
    
    laraflow:show reports Dispatched, Available (only when it differs — a
    delayed retry, with the reason spelled out), and Queue wait: open-ended
    ("1 min 30 s so far") while pending, exact once a worker owns it, and
    absent entirely for an in-process run rather than shown as a dash that
    would imply a queue was involved.
    
    BREAKING: ExecutionRepository gained recordDispatch(). Custom
    implementations must add it, must not claim-guard it, and must treat an
    availability earlier than the dispatch time as the dispatch time.
    Recorded in CHANGELOG.md and docs/public-api.md with the earlier
    Milestone 4 contract changes; docs/queue-and-recovery.md gains a Queue
    lag section with the full case table and a metrics-listener recipe.
    
    Verified in the playground against the real database queue: a pending
    execution showing its open wait, the same execution after a worker,
    a delayed retry showing its due-time availability, and an in-process
    run showing no queue rows at all.
    
