    Milestone 4 step 4: a stable, bounded listing API on the repository contract
    
    Applications and consoles can now list, filter, count, and page through
    executions without touching the internal Eloquent models.
    ExecutionRepository gains list(ExecutionFilter, $limit, $cursor),
    count(ExecutionFilter), and attemptHistory(WorkflowExecution), with the
    filter and page as public readonly value objects.
    
    ExecutionFilter offers exactly the criteria the step 2 indexes can
    serve — workflow(s), status(es), correlation id, tenant id, creation
    and finish windows, sort direction — and nothing else on purpose. The
    migration test pins an index behind each; a filter Laraflow cannot
    bound is a filter it does not expose, because an unindexed filter on a
    high-volume table is how observability tooling takes an application
    down. Windows are half-open (inclusive start, exclusive end) so
    consecutive windows neither overlap nor leave a gap, which reporting
    and retention both depend on. Blank identifiers mean unfiltered,
    mirroring ExecutionOptions, so a caller forwarding an optional input
    cannot end up with a filter that matches nothing. Inverted windows and
    wrong types fail at construction.
    
    Pagination is keyset, not offset. The cursor is the last id of a page
    and the next page continues strictly past it in the filter's direction,
    so a listing stays correct while rows are being inserted underneath it
    and costs the same on page 1000 as on page 1 — both proven by test.
    Fetching one row past the page tells the last page it is last without
    a second round trip. Page size is clamped to MAX_PAGE_SIZE, a constant
    on the contract rather than the implementation so no implementation
    can forget the bound, and the page reports the limit actually applied.
    A malformed cursor is rejected rather than guessed at.
    
    attemptHistory() reads every step's attempts in one query using the
    execution id the attempts table has denormalised since Milestone 3 for
    exactly this purpose, ordered by the owning step's position rather than
    by step id — the ids happen to ascend with position, but the order a
    caller sees should rest on the column that means it. list() and count()
    share one filter-to-SQL method so they can never disagree.
    
