    WorkflowRunner::cancel() and laraflow:cancel give the cancelled state
    from step 2 its runtime.
    
    Work nobody is running — pending, failed, retrying — is cancelled
    outright by a compare-and-set on status, the same predicate claiming
    uses, so cancellation cannot race a worker: whichever UPDATE lands
    first, the other is refused. Any scheduled retry is voided, and the
    cancelled execution is consequently invisible to the due-retry scan.
    
    A running execution is never rewritten underneath its worker. It gets
    cancel_requested_at, and the owning worker honours it at its next step
    boundary: the step in flight finishes and checkpoints, later steps stay
    pending, the claim is released, and work already completed is kept.
    Laraflow cannot interrupt a step mid-flight — bounding that belongs to
    the step's own deadline and the queue worker's timeout. A request made
    during the final step therefore does not prevent completion, because no
    later boundary exists.
    
    Cancelled is terminal everywhere: claim(), execute(),
    dispatchExecution(), and recovery all refuse it. Repeat cancellations
    are idempotent no-ops that fire no second event, keeping
    WorkflowExecutionCancelled's once-per-execution guarantee honest; a
    completed execution is refused with ExecutionNotCancellableException.
    
