    Milestone 3 step 3: step policy model and resolution
    
    StepPolicy declares retry and timeout policy for one step: a total
    attempt budget, exponential backoff capped at max_delay, retryOn /
    dontRetryOn matched by instanceof, and a per-attempt deadline. It is
    pure — it decides whether to retry and how long to wait, and knows
    nothing of persistence, queues, or clocks.
    
    Backoff jitter is derived by hashing a seed (the runner will pass the
    execution id and step position) rather than drawn from randomness, so
    one logical retry always yields the same delay — reproducible in tests,
    explainable in production — while different executions still spread
    apart. When the jitter window would cross max_delay it slides down to
    fit rather than being clipped against it: clipping landed every
    upward-jittered execution on exactly max_delay, bunching roughly half
    of them onto one instant in exactly the case jitter exists to prevent.
    
    Deterministic definition and serialization faults, and ClaimLost, are
    never retried whatever a policy says; another attempt could only repeat
    the outcome.
    
    Resolution is per step: the step's own HasStepPolicy wins, then its
    workflow's defaultStepPolicy(), then laraflow.retry / laraflow.timeout
    config — so one slow step can declare a longer budget than its
    siblings. The shipped default is max_attempts = 1, leaving retries
    opt-in. Workflow also gains failurePolicy(), defaulting to Stop.
    
    Nothing consumes any of this yet: the runner is untouched.
    
